        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A protein powder label is mostly protein, sweetener, thickener, emulsifier and flavor. Once you know what those lines do, the ones that deserve attention stand out: undisclosed blends, free amino acids padding the protein number, sugar alcohols your gut dislikes, extras with no stated dose and certification claims you have not verified.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Read the ingredient order, do the protein math and check certifications in the certifier's database. Then remember that the label cannot show contamination, so independent testing is the final word. This guide is general information, not medical advice; if you have allergies, kidney disease, are pregnant or take medication, check with your doctor or a registered dietitian.
          </p>
